---
import OrcaDocs from "../../../../components/OrcaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { ORCA_REPO } from "../../../../data/orca";
import { GHARCHIVE_REPO } from "../../../../data/gharchive";

const DOCS = `${ORCA_REPO}/blob/main/docs`;

const description =
  "Running some of Orca's pipelines on a second machine: what a remote worker is, a worker token, a placement, the worker container that needs only outbound HTTPS, the census as the example, and what doesn't run on workers yet.";
---

<OrcaDocs href="/s/orca/docs/workers" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">Remote workers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A remote worker runs a pipeline's runs on a second machine. It needs no database access
    and no inbound ports, only outbound HTTPS to the Orca UI and to the bucket.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#what">What it is</a>
    <a href="#setup">Setting one up</a>
    <a href="#census">The census</a>
    <a href="#limits">Limits</a>
    <a href="#gotchas">Docker Desktop</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="what">What it is</h2>
  <p>
    The scheduler stays the only brain: it fires, senses, queues and retries, and keeps all
    state in its Postgres. A pipeline registered with <code>placement = "worker:&lt;pool&gt;"</code>
    is never started by the scheduler. Its runs wait, queued, until a worker of that pool
    claims one. The worker polls the UI's HTTPS API (<code>/api/worker/*</code>), runs the
    pipeline's binary on its own machine, and the run records its state through the same
    API instead of Postgres. Data and logs go from the worker straight to the warehouse and
    logs buckets.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the reason to use one: a second machine with spare CPU, memory or disk
    without exposing Postgres to it, and the way to scale out when the first machine is
    full. A worker holds a token scoped to its pool and nothing else. A worker that dies has its
    runs queued again.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="wd-t wd-d" class="wdiagram">
      <title id="wd-t">A remote worker and the server</title>
      <desc id="wd-d">
        The worker polls the UI over HTTPS to claim runs and record their state; the UI and
        scheduler use Postgres. Server and worker both read and write the bucket directly.
      </desc>
      <defs>
        <marker id="wd-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" class="wd-head" />
        </marker>
      </defs>
      <rect x="10" y="20" width="270" height="170" rx="10" class="wd-box" />
      <text x="24" y="42" class="wd-cap">server</text>
      <rect x="30" y="56" width="110" height="34" rx="6" class="wd-node" />
      <text x="85" y="78" class="wd-txt">scheduler</text>
      <rect x="30" y="111" width="110" height="34" rx="6" class="wd-node" />
      <text x="85" y="133" class="wd-txt">orca ui</text>
      <rect x="170" y="56" width="90" height="34" rx="6" class="wd-node" />
      <text x="215" y="78" class="wd-txt">Postgres</text>
      <path d="M140 73 L170 73" class="wd-line" marker-end="url(#wd-a)" />
      <path d="M120 111 L190 90" class="wd-line" marker-end="url(#wd-a)" />

      <rect x="380" y="20" width="250" height="170" rx="10" class="wd-box" />
      <text x="394" y="42" class="wd-cap">worker</text>
      <rect x="400" y="111" width="210" height="34" rx="6" class="wd-node" />
      <text x="505" y="133" class="wd-txt">orca worker --pool bgent</text>
      <text x="505" y="166" class="wd-sub">runs the pipeline's binary</text>

      <path d="M140 128 L400 128" class="wd-line wd-https" marker-start="url(#wd-a)" marker-end="url(#wd-a)" />
      <text x="330" y="118" class="wd-sub">HTTPS</text>

      <rect x="150" y="212" width="340" height="30" rx="6" class="wd-node" />
      <text x="320" y="232" class="wd-txt">bucket: warehouse and logs</text>
      <path d="M85 190 L85 227 L150 227" class="wd-line" marker-end="url(#wd-a)" />
      <path d="M505 190 L505 227 L490 227" class="wd-line" marker-end="url(#wd-a)" />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      The only path from the worker to the server is HTTPS to the UI (claims, state calls, heartbeats). Postgres is never
      reachable from the worker.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="setup">Setting one up</h2>
  <p>
    Deploy the server first. Then four steps: a token, a placement, the worker, and a look
    at Ops.
  </p>

  <h3>1. A token, on the server</h3>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`orca worker-token create --pool bgent --label bgent   # prints the token, once
orca worker-token list
orca worker-token revoke 3`} />
  <p>
    The token is scoped to one pool and expires (by default after a year at most). The
    server keeps only its hash. Copy it to the worker's machine as a file.
  </p>

  <h3>2. A placement, in orca.toml</h3>
  <CodeBlock label="orca.toml" code={`[pipelines.mojo_history]
placement = "worker:bgent"
env = { DISTA_WORKER_SLOTS = "3" }`} />
  <p>
    Or <code>orca register --placement worker:bgent</code>. Register again after the
    change. The pipeline's page then says its runs are waiting for a worker of the pool.
  </p>

  <h3>3. The worker</h3>
  <p>
    The simplest worker is the server's own image at the same tag, so the pipeline
    binaries are at the paths they were registered with. It needs the pool, the UI's URL,
    the token file and the bucket's settings:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`docker run -d --name orca-worker --restart always \\
  --env-file s3.env -v orca-worker-secrets:/etc/orca/w:ro \\
  -e ORCA_WAREHOUSE=s3://<bucket>/warehouse -e ORCA_LOGS=s3://<bucket>/logs \\
  -e AWS_REGION=<location> -e AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3=https://<location>.your-objectstorage.com \\
  ghcr.io/magmalake/orca:<tag> worker --pool bgent --max-runs 3 \\
    --remote https://orca.example.com --token-file /etc/orca/w/token`} />
  <p>
    <code>--max-runs</code> is how many runs the worker takes at once. A worker with
    <code>ORCA_DATABASE_URL</code> set refuses to start. Since a worker admits runs
    against its own host, its log says what it can grant:
    <code>grants up to 8 CPUs and 11421 MB</code>, less <code>ORCA_RESERVE_CPUS</code>
    and <code>ORCA_RESERVE_MEMORY_MB</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Check Ops</h3>
  <p>
    Ops, then Workers, (<code>/api/workers</code> as JSON) lists each token with its pool,
    the worker's name and image, its last heartbeat, its current runs, CPUs and memory.
    A run's page says which worker ran it. <code>docker logs orca-worker</code> shows each
    run it starts and how it exited.
  </p>

  <h2 id="census">The census</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={GHARCHIVE_REPO} rel="noopener">The census</a> runs on a Hetzner CAX21: the
    scheduler, the UI, Postgres, the site, and gharchive's fill, mojo_repos and mojo_stats.
    The git-heavy pipelines, mojo_history, mojo_versions and mojo_activity, run on a second
    machine, bgent, an Apple-silicon Mac running Docker. It is arm64 like the CAX21, so it
    runs the same image. Each of the three is registered like this:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="orca.toml" code={`[pipelines.mojo_history]
placement = "worker:bgent"
env = { DISTA_WORKER_SLOTS = "3" }`} />
  <p>
    The worker is the container above, with the census's bucket and one more setting,
    <code>MOJO_HISTORY_WORKDIR=/work/clones</code> on a volume, for the clones. The
    <a href={`${GHARCHIVE_REPO}#a-second-machine`} rel="noopener">census README</a> has
    the exact commands.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Fills.</strong> <code>fills = "all"</code> doesn't run on worker pools yet
      (<a href={`${ORCA_REPO}/issues/177`} rel="noopener">orca #177</a>). Use
      <code>orca backfill</code> for a pool's partitions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The same tag.</strong> The worker runs the code of its own image. Deploy the
      server first, then restart the worker on the same tag.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Secrets.</strong> A pipeline with secrets needs
      <code>ORCA_SECRET_FETCH</code> on the worker, to read them there. The spec names
      the secrets and never carries their values.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The UI's port.</strong> By default the worker API shares the UI's port, so a
      slow page can delay a worker. From orca 0.15.0, <code>ORCA_WORKER_ADDR</code> serves
      it on a listener of its own.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="gotchas">Docker Desktop and the token</h2>
  <p>
    Docker Desktop runs containers in a virtual machine, which may not see a file
    bind-mounted from under <code>~/.config</code>: the mount shows up as an empty
    directory, and the worker finds no token. Put the token in a Docker volume instead:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`docker run --rm -i --entrypoint sh -v orca-worker-secrets:/s \\
  ghcr.io/magmalake/orca:<tag> -c 'cat > /s/token && chmod 600 /s/token' < worker-token`} />
  <p>
    The worker container above mounts that volume read-only at <code>/etc/orca/w</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="more">More</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={`${DOCS}/workers.md`} rel="noopener">workers.md</a> in the Orca repository has
    the flags, how a dead worker's runs are retried, the state calls and the network
    details. <a href="/s/orca/docs/run#placement">Scheduling and running</a> says where
    placement fits among the runners.
  </p>
</OrcaDocs>

